A||||4||Brian84SE||headlights||||1||
Z||000000||Brian84SE||02-08-2000||09:02 PM||||I just recently purchased my way cool 84 Fiero and like most others I am having headlight problems.  The headlights neither go up nor go down.  The tailight fuse is good .  I dont seem to be getting power at the isolation relay(the white wire).  Is there a fusible link I'm missing somewhere?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||daved||02-08-2000||10:19 PM||||Brian, Pull the light switch and see if the white wire is hot there; if not try the orange wire, as it comes directly from the fuse. Note that the white wire is only hot when the light switch is off!<BR>If you have power at the orange wire, and not at the white with the switch off, then the switch is defective.<BR>Be careful. The red wire is hot at all times, and is protected by a fusible link only...a real bother to replace.  My guess would be the switch, as its the moving part. Hope you find the problem. D.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ggarsk||02-09-2000||07:58 PM||||Instead of opening another thread on headlights,I thought I would use this one. I rebuilt both motors and now the pass side works perfectly,but the drivers side doesn't. When I connect the blue wire the light comes up even with the lights off. That is all it does. The headlight relays are good. Any thoughts on this prob. thanks,ggarsk.||||reg||1
Z||000003||AL||02-09-2000||09:53 PM||||try this,i did the same thing rebuilt my motors, well the drivers side would work fine but the right side wouldn't. so then i connected the rght headlight to the lft head light connector andbingo it worked. so  figured that the orange wire wasn't giving me power so i connected another wre to te other orange wire, on the drivers side and now bot work, so check yor wires, good luck, AL.   ||||reg||1
Z||000004||daved||02-09-2000||10:15 PM||||The headlight (actuator) relay is the device that changes the motor rotation, and thus determines which direction the headlight motor turns. The blue wire is the power for the close direction only, so the only way for the motor to get power is from the 'open' circuit, through the relay.<BR>I assume you didnt monkey with the brushes or wiring to the torque(limit)switches while you had it apart, and got them crossed somehow?  It is possible to make the motor go the wrong direction if the wires were switched.<BR>Try swapping the headlight (actuator) relays. If it still doesn't work, then I'm afraid it must be the motor itself.||||reg||1
